          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "7e2ba909-aac0-520c-bec8-c84186a85d6c",
          ":answer-field/key": "III",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "fb5b601c-e834-5931-87ab-ae4dfa8e3000",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "false"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "b3e8ecbd-1ad5-552a-b241-47b0c897af01",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "true"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "fb5b601c-e834-5931-87ab-ae4dfa8e3000"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi$. Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,-1]\\cup[1,\\infty)$.  Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- The range is unbounded above and below, so the function has no global minimum or maximum. Hence, statement III is false.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
    },
